paragraphs.text:
To keep throughout the metaphorical character of this verse, it ought to be rendered thus, — For the bruising of the daughter of my people I was bruised, I became black;
Desolation possessed me. But taking the words as applied to the mind, divested of metaphor, we must render them thus, — For the sorrow of the daughter of my people I sorrowed, I mourned;
Astonishment possessed me. And this “astonishment” he explains in the next verse: there were means of restoration, and yet the people were not restored; at this he was astonished. — Ed.
